\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\looseness=-1 We evaluate ACR-VLA on three frozen flow-matching VLAs spanning 0.4B to 7B parameters across four LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero} task suites, asking: (i)~does the pipeline generalize across VLA scales and architectures? (ii)~does the $K{=}2$ uncertainty probe enable adaptive compute allocation? (iii)~what is the relative contribution of selection vs.\ correction?

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}

\paragraph{Benchmarks.}
We use all four LIBERO suites: Object (10 tasks, novel object instances), Spatial (10 tasks, spatial reasoning), Goal (10 tasks, goal specification), and Long (10 tasks, long-horizon manipulation). Each task is evaluated over 10 rollouts (max 300 steps); success is binary.

\paragraph{VLAs under test.}
\looseness=-1 Three frozen flow-matching VLAs serve as base policies:
\textbf{SmolVLA}~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (0.4B, SigLIP+Idefics3 vision encoder, 960D observation features) with dual-camera RGB ($256{\times}256$) and 8D proprioceptive state;
\textbf{$\pi_{0.5}$}~\cite{black2024pi0} (3.6B, PaliGemma2+Gemma2, 2048D features, 50-step action chunking);
\textbf{OpenVLA-OFT}~\cite{kim2024openvla} (7B, flow-matching variant, near-deterministic at $T{=}0.5$---included as an applicability boundary test, Appendix~\ref{sec:applicability}).
Actions are 7D (6D pose + 1D gripper); the gripper is thresholded outside the pipeline.

\paragraph{CRM training.}
\looseness=-1 A single CRM is trained per VLA on all four LIBERO suites (in-distribution): 10 demonstrations $\times$ 40 tasks, $K{=}10$ forward passes each, Adam $\mathrm{lr}{=}10^{-3}$, batch 32, 50 epochs, $\varepsilon{=}0.2$. The CRM architecture is identical across VLAs (${\sim}$327K parameters); only the input projection adapts to the base VLA's observation feature dimensionality.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
All baselines use the same frozen VLA with no weight modification: \textbf{VLA-only} ($K{=}1$, no correction), \textbf{Random} ($K{=}5{/}10$, uniform sample, no CRM), and \textbf{Argmin} ($K{=}5{/}10$, $L_2$ selection, no CRM). We report seed-level Mann-Whitney U tests (two-sided); 3--9 seeds per condition.

\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

\looseness=-1 Table~\ref{tab:main} reports task success rates across all three VLAs and four LIBERO suites. On SmolVLA, Plan~A ($K{=}2$ selective CRM) achieves $18.3 \pm 2.1$\% on Object ($+$7.5\,pp vs.\ VLA-only) and $35.0 \pm 1.0$\% on Spatial ($+$5.6\,pp), exceeding fixed-$K{=}10$ unconditional correction ($16.8$\%, $34.4$\%) with 80\% fewer VLA forward passes and matching $K{=}20$ ($18.8$\%, $34.3$\%) at 90\% fewer (Appendix~\ref{sec:k_scaling}). The CRM trigger rate is task-difficulty-dependent: 83.7\% on Object (16.3\% fast-path steps) vs.\ 97.6\% on Spatial, where correction is almost universally beneficial.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Task success rate (\%\,$\uparrow$) across three flow-matching VLAs and four LIBERO suites. Plan~A: $K{=}2$ selective CRM (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k_method}). Mean $\pm$ std over $n$ seeds. SmolVLA Object/Spatial: $n{=}9{/}5$ (VLA-only), $n{=}3$ (Plan~A). $\pi_{0.5}$: $n{=}3$. \placeholder{}: evaluation in progress.}
\label{tab:main}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
VLA & Method & Object (\%) & Spatial (\%) & Goal (\%) & Long (\%) \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{SmolVLA (0.4B)} & VLA-only & $10.8 \pm 1.3$ & $29.4 \pm 1.5$ & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} \\
 & \textbf{Plan~A} & $\mathbf{18.3 \pm 2.1}$ & $\mathbf{35.0 \pm 1.0}$ & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{$\pi_{0.5}$ (3.6B)} & VLA-only & $81.5 \pm 0.5$ & $84.2 \pm 1.0$ & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} \\
 & \textbf{Plan~A} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{OVA-OFT (7B)} & VLA-only & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} \\
 & \textbf{Plan~A} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\looseness=-1 $\pi_{0.5}$ operates at $81$--$85$\% baseline, substantially above SmolVLA's $11$--$29$\%, leaving limited headroom for post-hoc correction. This tests whether the pipeline helps when the base policy is already strong; Plan~A results are pending. OpenVLA-OFT produces near-deterministic actions ($8/10$ identical samples at $T{=}0.5$), serving as an applicability boundary test for the stochastic diversity prerequisite (Appendix~\ref{sec:applicability}). Goal and Long evaluations with the unified CRM are in progress.

\subsection{Adaptive Inference-Time Compute}
\label{sec:adaptive_k}

\looseness=-1 The key question is whether compute can be allocated per step rather than using a fixed $K$. We validate the $K{=}2$ $L_2$ probe (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k_method}) and evaluate threshold-based adaptive allocation on SmolVLA.

\paragraph{Uncertainty signal validation.}
The pairwise $L_2$ distance from $K{=}2$ samples correlates near-perfectly with the full $K{=}10$ sample variance ($\rho{=}0.9979$, Spearman; Figure~\ref{fig:k2_correlation}). Three alternative signals fail: flow-matching velocity divergence (FMVD, $\rho{=}0.08$), CRM correction magnitude at $K{=}1$ ($\rho{=}0.15$), and CRM correction magnitude at $K{=}2$ ($\rho{=}0.13$). The $K{=}2$ $L_2$ distance is the only viable per-step uncertainty proxy among the four candidates tested.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig5_k2_correlation.pdf}
\caption{Per-step uncertainty signals vs.\ $K{=}10$ sample variance. (a)~$K{=}2$ $L_2$ pairwise distance achieves $\rho{=}0.9979$, validating it as a near-perfect proxy. (b)~FMVD ($\rho{=}0.08$) shows no correlation.}
\label{fig:k2_correlation}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Threshold sweep.}
\looseness=-1 Table~\ref{tab:adaptive} reports performance on LIBERO-Object. Plan~A ($K{=}2$ selective CRM) Pareto-dominates all variable-$K$ thresholds: $18.3$\% at avg\_$K{=}2$, exceeding fixed $K{=}10$ ($16.8$\%) with 80\% fewer VLA forward passes. The optimal threshold is task-difficulty-dependent: on Spatial (easier), P90 reaches $31.5$\% at avg\_$K{=}3.55$ while on Object (harder) P50 is the sweet spot (Appendix~\ref{sec:spatial_adaptive}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Adaptive $K$ threshold sweep and Plan~A on LIBERO-Object ($n{=}3$ seeds unless noted). Plan~A Pareto-dominates all variable-$K$ thresholds. $^\S$\,$n{=}6$. $^\|$\,$n{=}9$.}
\label{tab:adaptive}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
$\tau$ & SR (\%) & avg\_$K$ & fast path (\%) & SR/SR$_{K{=}10}$ \\
\midrule
\textbf{Plan~A (ours)} & $\mathbf{18.3 \pm 2.1}$ & \textbf{2.0} & \textbf{16.3} & \textbf{109\%} \\
\midrule
P90 & $13.3 \pm 1.2$ & 2.62 & 92.2 & 79\% \\
P75 & $13.7 \pm 2.1$ & 3.08 & 86.5 & 82\% \\
P50 & $15.3 \pm 1.2$ & 3.81 & 77.3 & 91\% \\
P25 & $14.3 \pm 1.2$ & 4.79 & 65.1 & 85\% \\
\midrule
Fixed $K{=}10^\S$ & $16.8 \pm 1.8$ & 10.0 & 0 & 100\% \\
VLA-only$^{\|}$ & $10.8 \pm 1.3$ & 1.0 & --- & 64\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Component Analysis}
\label{sec:magnitude_results}
\label{sec:failure_modes}

\looseness=-2 The following analysis on SmolVLA validates key design decisions in the ACR-VLA pipeline.

\paragraph{Selection vs.\ correction.}
Table~\ref{tab:decomp} isolates each component at fixed $K$. Random selection fails to exceed VLA-only on either benchmark (Object $9.7$\% vs.\ $10.8$\%; Spatial $29.3$\% vs.\ $29.4$\%), confirming that sampling more actions without ranking is insufficient. Argmin adds $+$1.3\,pp at $K{=}10$; CRM correction contributes the remaining $+$4.5\,pp (78\% of the total gain). At $K{=}5$, correction contributes 68\%. At $K{=}1$, CRM falls below baseline ($10.3$\% vs.\ $10.8$\%), validating the $K{\geq}2$ requirement that motivated the adaptive probe.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Component decomposition on SmolVLA (LIBERO-Object and Spatial). Mean $\pm$ std; $n$: Object seeds. Spatial $n$: 5 (VLA-only), 4 ($K{=}5$), 3 (Random $K{=}10$, CRM $K{=}10$, Plan~A). Top: proposed method; middle/bottom: analysis baselines.}
\label{tab:decomp}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Method & $K$ & Object (\%) & $n$ & Spatial (\%) & $\Delta$ Obj \\
\midrule
\textbf{Plan~A (ours)} & \textbf{2} & $\mathbf{18.3 \pm 2.1}$ & \textbf{3} & $\mathbf{35.0 \pm 1.0}$ & $\mathbf{+7.5}$ \\
\midrule
VLA-only & 1 & $10.8 \pm 1.3$ & 9 & $29.4 \pm 1.5$ & --- \\
Random & 5 & $10.5 \pm 2.9$ & 4 & --- & $-0.3$ \\
Argmin & 5 & $13.0 \pm 1.6$ & 4 & --- & $+2.2$ \\
CRM obs\_only & 5 & $16.0 \pm 2.2$ & 7 & $34.5 \pm 2.1$ & $+5.2$ \\
\midrule
Random & 10 & $9.7 \pm 1.5$ & 6 & $29.3 \pm 4.2$ & $-1.1$ \\
Argmin & 10 & $12.3 \pm 2.6$ & 6 & --- & $+1.5$ \\
CRM obs\_only & 10 & $16.8 \pm 1.8$ & 6 & $34.4 \pm 1.5$ & $+6.0$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Conditioning and magnitude.}
\looseness=-2 The CRM conditioning interacts with $\varepsilon$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:magnitude_appendix}): obs\_only is stable across all $\varepsilon$ (12.5--16.8\%); perdof collapses progressively (${\sim}$14\% $\to$ 0\%) because $K{=}10$ in $d{=}7$ yields effectively rank-deficient covariance and large $\varepsilon$ amplifies noisy per-DOF estimates; scalar (8.5\%) falls below VLA-only. These results motivated the obs\_only conditioning as the primary CRM variant.

\paragraph{Applicability boundaries.}
\looseness=-1 Three distribution shifts define further boundaries: (1)~input ($K{=}1$ CRM, 10.3\%), (2)~task (near-OOD within-suite held-out tasks: $-$12\,pp; far-OOD cross-suite: neutral, $p{=}0.46$), (3)~feature (perdof $\to$ 0\%). All share a mechanism: CRM degrades when inputs depart from training distribution (Appendix~\ref{sec:per_task}). Plan~A runs $1.82{\times}$ faster than fixed $K{=}10$ with 0.28\,ms CRM overhead (Appendix~\ref{sec:latency}).

